1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,760
Scalp odor that won't go away even after washing?

2
00:00:04,080 --> 00:00:06,320
Pay attention to these three habits.

3
00:00:06,640 --> 00:00:09,840
Your scalp has more oil glands than your face.

4
00:00:10,160 --> 00:00:22,160
Within four to twelve hours after washing, that oil starts to oxidize, and by twelve to twenty-four hours bacteria and yeast break it down into odor compounds.

5
00:00:22,480 --> 00:00:25,520
These three habits speed that process up.

6
00:00:25,840 --> 00:00:30,480
First is relying on dry shampoo instead of a real wash.

7
00:00:30,800 --> 00:00:38,800
It masks oil temporarily, but the residue builds up and traps oxidizing sebum right against the scalp.

8
00:00:39,120 --> 00:00:41,680
Second is rinsing too fast.

9
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:48,720
Leftover shampoo and conditioner residue mixes with sebum and breaks down into a stronger odor.

10
00:00:49,040 --> 00:00:51,680
Third is using a dirty hairbrush.

11
00:00:52,000 --> 00:01:03,040
An unwashed brush can hold thousands of bacteria colonies per square inch, and every stroke sends that oil and bacteria straight back onto clean hair.

12
00:01:03,360 --> 00:01:08,800
Rinse dry shampoo out every few uses instead of skipping a wash entirely.

13
00:01:09,120 --> 00:01:15,440
Spend a full minute rinsing shampoo right at the scalp, not just through the length of your hair.

14
00:01:15,760 --> 00:01:19,520
And wash your hairbrush weekly with warm, soapy water.

15
00:01:19,840 --> 00:01:23,840
These three changes make scalp odor noticeably better.
